Article 9 In government procurement activities, procurement personnel and relevant persons who have any of the following interests with a supplier shall withdraw:
(1) having an employment relationship with the supplier within the 3 years prior to participating in the procurement activity;
(2) having served as a director or supervisor of the supplier within the 3 years prior to participating in the procurement activity;
(3) being a controlling shareholder or actual controller of the supplier within the 3 years prior to participating in the procurement activity;
(4) being the spouse, lineal relative by blood, collateral relative by blood within three generations, or close relative by marriage of the legal representative or responsible person of the supplier;
(5) having other relationships with the supplier that may affect the fairness and impartiality of the government procurement activity.
Where a supplier believes that procurement personnel or relevant persons have interests with other suppliers, it may submit a written application for withdrawal to the procuring entity or procurement agency, stating the reasons. The procuring entity or procurement agency shall promptly inquire of the person subject to the withdrawal application; if such person has an interest, he or she shall withdraw.

Article 17 A supplier participating in a government procurement activity shall meet the conditions prescribed in Paragraph 1 of Article 22 of the Government Procurement Law and provide the following materials:
(1) the business license or other certification documents for a legal person or other organization, and the identity certificate for a natural person;
(2) financial status reports, and relevant materials on tax payment and social security fund contributions according to law;
(3) evidentiary materials proving it has the equipment and professional technical capabilities necessary for performing the contract;
(4) a written statement that it has no record of major illegal acts in its business operations within the 3 years prior to participating in the government procurement activity;
(5) evidentiary materials proving it meets other conditions prescribed by laws and administrative regulations.
Where the procurement project has special requirements, the supplier shall also provide evidentiary materials or explanations that it meets such special requirements.

Article 20 Where a procuring entity or procurement agency has any of the following circumstances, it constitutes differential treatment or discriminatory treatment against suppliers by means of unreasonable conditions:
(1) providing suppliers with differentiated project information in respect of the same procurement project;
(2) the eligibility, technical, or commercial conditions set are incompatible with the specific characteristics and actual needs of the procurement project, or are unrelated to the performance of the contract;
(3) the technical, service or other requirements in the procurement needs target a specific supplier or specific product;
(4) using performance or awards of a specific administrative region or specific industry as a condition for scoring or for winning a bid or concluding a transaction;
(5) applying different qualification review or evaluation standards to suppliers;
(6) restricting or designating specific patents, trademarks, brands, or suppliers;
(7) illegally restricting the form of ownership, form of organization, or location of suppliers;
(8) restricting or excluding potential suppliers by other unreasonable conditions.

Article 53 The date on which a supplier should have known that its rights and interests have been impaired as prescribed in Article 52 of the Government Procurement Law means:
(1) for a challenge raised against challengeable procurement documents, the date of receipt of the procurement documents or the date of expiry of the announcement period of the procurement documents;
(2) for a challenge raised against the procurement process, the date of conclusion of each procurement procedure stage;
(3) for a challenge raised against the result of winning the bid or concluding the transaction, the date of expiry of the announcement period of the result of the winning bid or successful transaction.

Article 60 In addition to the assessment matters specified in Article 66 of the Government Procurement Law, the assessment matters of the finance department with respect to centralized procurement agencies also include:
(1) implementation of government procurement policies;
(2) quality of preparation of procurement documents;
(3) implementation of procurement methods and procurement procedures;
(4) responses to inquiries and challenges;
(5) establishment and implementation of internal supervision and management systems;
(6) other matters prescribed by the finance departments of the people's governments at or above the provincial level.
The finance department shall formulate an assessment plan and conduct regular assessments of centralized procurement agencies; where the assessment results reveal important matters, it shall report to the people's government at the same level.

Article 67 Where a procuring entity has any of the following circumstances, the finance department shall order it to make corrections within a specified time limit, give it a warning, impose sanctions on the person directly in charge and other directly responsible persons in accordance with law, and make a public notice:
(1) failing to formulate a government procurement implementation plan in accordance with the provisions, or failing to submit the government procurement implementation plan to the finance department of the people's government at the same level for record in accordance with the provisions;
(2) breaking down a project that should be subject to public tendering into smaller parts or using any other means to circumvent public tendering;
(3) failing to determine the winning or successful supplier from among the candidates for winning the bid or concluding the transaction recommended by the bid evaluation committee, competitive negotiation team, or quotation team in accordance with the provisions;
(4) failing to sign a government procurement contract in accordance with the matters specified in the procurement documents;
(5) during the performance of a government procurement contract, adding an amount of procurement of goods, works, or services identical to the subject matter of the contract that exceeds 10% of the original contract procurement amount;
(6) altering, suspending, or terminating a government procurement contract without authorization;
(7) failing to announce a government procurement contract in accordance with the provisions;
(8) failing to submit the duplicate of a government procurement contract to the finance department of the people's government at the same level and relevant departments for record within the prescribed time.

Article 68 Where a procuring entity or procurement agency has any of the following circumstances, it shall be investigated for legal liability in accordance with Articles 71 and 78 of the Government Procurement Law:
(1) failing to implement procurement in the manner prescribed by the Government Procurement Law and these Regulations;
(2) failing to publish information on a government procurement project on the designated media in accordance with law;
(3) failing to implement government procurement policies in accordance with the provisions;
(4) violating the provisions of Article 15 of these Regulations, resulting in the inability to organize the acceptance inspection of the supplier's performance or causing losses to State property;
(5) failing to select evaluation experts from the government procurement evaluation expert database in accordance with law;
(6) illegally interfering in procurement evaluation activities;
(7) when adopting the comprehensive score method, the score allocation in the evaluation criteria does not correspond to the quantified indicators of the evaluation factors;
(8) failing to respond to a supplier's inquiry or challenge within the prescribed time limit;
(9) altering the evaluation results by means such as testing samples or inspecting suppliers;
(10) failing to organize the acceptance inspection of the supplier's performance in accordance with the provisions.

Article 69 Where a centralized procurement agency has any of the following circumstances, the finance department shall order it to make corrections within a specified time limit, give it a warning, and if there are illegal gains, shall also confiscate the illegal gains; impose sanctions on the person directly in charge and other directly responsible persons in accordance with law, and make a public notice:
(1) the internal supervision and management system is not sound, and the positions and personnel that should be separately established and separated according to law are not separately established or separated;
(2) entrusting a centralized procurement project to another procurement agency for procurement;
(3) engaging in profit-making activities.

Article 72 Where a supplier has any of the following circumstances, it shall be investigated for legal liability in accordance with Paragraph 1 of Article 77 of the Government Procurement Law:
(1) bribing members of the bid evaluation committee, competitive negotiation team, or quotation team, or providing other improper benefits;
(2) after winning the bid or concluding the transaction, refusing without justifiable reasons to sign a government procurement contract with the procuring entity;
(3) failing to sign a government procurement contract in accordance with the matters specified in the procurement documents;
(4) subcontracting a government procurement contract;
(5) providing counterfeit or shoddy products;
(6) altering, suspending, or terminating a government procurement contract without authorization.
Where a supplier has the circumstance specified in Item (1) of the preceding paragraph, the winning bid or successful transaction shall be void. Where a supplier's eligibility changes during the evaluation stage and it fails to notify the procuring entity and procurement agency in accordance with Article 21 of these Regulations, a fine of 5‰ of the procurement amount shall be imposed, the supplier shall be included in the list of records of不良 conduct, and the winning bid or successful transaction shall be void.

Article 74 Any of the following circumstances constitutes malicious collusion, and the supplier shall be investigated for legal liability in accordance with Paragraph 1 of Article 77 of the Government Procurement Law, and the procuring entity, procurement agency, and their staff members shall be investigated for legal liability in accordance with Article 72 of the Government Procurement Law:
(1) a supplier directly or indirectly obtains from the procuring entity or procurement agency relevant information about another supplier and modifies its own bid document or response document;
(2) a supplier, at the instigation of the procuring entity or procurement agency, replaces or modifies its bid document or response document;
(3) suppliers consult with each other on substantive content of bid documents or response documents such as prices and technical proposals;
(4) suppliers that are members of the same group, association, chamber of commerce or other organization participate in the government procurement activity in a coordinated manner as required by such organization;
(5) suppliers pre-arrange that a particular supplier will win the bid or conclude the transaction;
(6) suppliers agree among themselves that some suppliers will forego participation in the government procurement activity or forego winning the bid or concluding the transaction;
(7) other collusive conduct between a supplier and the procuring entity or procurement agency, or among suppliers, to seek a particular supplier to win the bid or conclude the transaction, or to exclude other suppliers.
